general_comments.body:
@LeeLenaleee Wow, thanks for pointing it out! I somehow missed that one 😅

I'm fully ready to abandon my code (haha, it's just a dozen lines of very trivial code). However, I think there's a lot of value in a plugin that is bundled with Chart.js. It would help drop the bar for those who are getting started.

What do you think about bundling @kurkle's plugin?

@LeeLenaleee @kurkle @stockiNail @dangreen Thanks a lot for your comments and help!

@kurkle I've removed references to individual plugins and added a link to [awesome](https://github.com/chartjs/awesome). However, I think that the plugins in the awesome list can be rearranged for the ease of use, hence this PR: https://github.com/chartjs/awesome/pull/52

@LeeLenaleee I've pinged `colorschemes` author on [GitHub](https://github.com/nagix/chartjs-plugin-colorschemes/pull/34#issuecomment-1285469120) and [Twitter](https://twitter.com/igorlukanin/status/1583074591387176967). Hopefully, it would help bring this plugin back on track!

@stockiNail Thanks for great [suggestions](https://github.com/chartjs/Chart.js/pull/10764#discussion_r1000527850)! I hope my reasoning for not introducing them in this plugin make sense.

Looks like the updated docs page and the plugin is ready for the final review 😄

@kurkle Yes, I also agree that there should be a more technically correct way of doing this. Unfortunately, I am not familiar with the codebase enough to figure that out.

Regarding what is `_visualValues`, I do not understand your classification of it being `just a copy of what was previously read`. It does store a calculated result that seems to be lost in the next iteration. I interpret it as a bridge to bring the effect of `minBarLength` into the `applyStack()` function, which otherwise needs to do a `O(n^2)` computation to find the correct value, or we will have to do some complex calculations (the technically correct way) in the controller function.

Finally, which `other values` are you referring to? If you mean `_visualValues`, then no, they do not serve for any other purposes.

